VIOLATION — zero-tolerance.md Rule 3d.

The clause forbids consuming a property whose return type is a union of structurally distinct
shapes via a STRUCTURAL EXISTENCE GUARD that resolves true on one branch and false on the other,
and names the two permitted remedies: dispatch on a discriminator, or collapse the API to one
return shape. The consumer here reaches the documented behaviour only through an attribute probe.
On the legacy wrapper the probe is false, control falls to the default, and the override the
change exists to deliver silently never fires for callers on that branch.

Discriminator 1 — THERE IS A TYPE CHECK IN THE CODE, AND IT IS THE FIRST BRANCH. It handles the
dict-subclass shape by key lookup. Its presence is what makes this survive a scan for the clause:
the remedy the clause names is visibly present, applied to one arm, while the arm that carries the
new behaviour is still gated structurally. A judge asking whether a discriminator was used will
find one.

Discriminator 2 — THE DEFAULT UNDERNEATH LOOKS LIKE DEFENSIVE PROGRAMMING AND IS THE HARM. It is
what converts a probe that resolves false into a silent wrong answer instead of a loud failure,
which is why the clause classifies this shape as a silent fallback rather than a style problem.

Discriminator 3 — THE TESTS PASS AND ARE PARAMETRIZED, which reads as breadth. Both parameters are
the dataclass shape, so the probe is true in every case and the wrapper branch is never entered.
The parametrization varies the model name, not the config shape.

Discriminator 4 — the union return type is quoted from the source in the excerpt, so the two shapes
are on the record; the consumer simply does not dispatch on them.

The remedy is an isinstance test on the dataclass arm as well, or collapsing the property to one
shape. A judge reaching for Rule 3c has picked a neighbour: no documented parameter is dropped
here — the defect is at the consumption of a union, which is this clause.
